{"name":"Earthen Sienna","hex":"#d1ac71","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"earthen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of earth or mud.","Made of clay."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["to provide or add soil to","to make earthly or earthlike"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earthen"},{"term":"sienna","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A form of clay containing iron and manganese.","A pigment with a reddish-brown colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["having a reddish-brown colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sienna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Earth structure","blocks":[{"text":"An earth structure is a building or other structure made largely from soil. Since soil is a widely available material, it has been used in construction since prehistory. It may be combined with other materials, compressed and/or baked to add strength."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Kharanagh-minaret.jpg/330px-Kharanagh-minaret.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_structure"},{"title":"Sienna","blocks":[{"text":"Sienna is an earth pigment containing iron oxide and manganese oxide. In its natural state, it is yellowish brown, and it is called raw sienna. When heated, it becomes a reddish brown, and it is called burnt sienna. It takes its name from the city-state of Siena, where it was produced during the Renaissance. Along with ochre and umber, it was one of the first pigments to be used by humans, and is found in many cave paintings. Since the Renaissance, it has been one of the brown pigments most widely used by artists."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sienna"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/House_in_Toteil_002.jpg/500px-House_in_Toteil_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https